<workout_file>
    <author>Tom Jelen</author>
    <name>HT Adaptation weeks 3-4</name>
    <activitySaveName>High Torque: Adaptation weeks 3-4 4x4 min 80-85% FTP @ 50-60 rpm</activitySaveName>
    <description>Adaptation weeks 3–4. Four 4-minute intervals at 80–85% FTP and 50–60 rpm, with 4 minutes of easy spin between them. Seated throughout. A labelled composite of three coaches: the cadence and intensity follow Neal Henderson's advice for riders just starting torque work (tempo efforts at 75–85% of threshold at 50–60 rpm, Fast Talk podcast ep. 125, 2020, machine transcript); Peter Schep (EF Pro Cycling) starts riders with &quot;60 rpms at 80 to 85% of your FTP&quot; for 15 minutes; the four 4-minute reps with rest as long as the rep come from John Wakefield (That Triathlon Show #465 podcast, machine transcript). The file holds 80%, the bottom of the range. The 4-week adaptation (4×4 at 60–65 rpm in weeks 1–2, then this session in weeks 3–4, one session a week) is our arrangement.</description>
    <sportType>bike</sportType>
    <tags />
    <workout>
        <Warmup Duration="900" PowerLow="0.40" PowerHigh="0.80">
            <textevent timeoffset="5" duration="15" message="Warm up at your normal cadence. No low cadence until the first interval." />
            <textevent timeoffset="30" duration="30" message="Source: Neal Henderson (Fast Talk ep. 125, 2020, machine transcript). For riders starting torque work: tempo efforts of 3-5 min at 75-85% of threshold, 50-60 rpm." />
            <textevent timeoffset="90" duration="20" message="Session logger and full workout calendar at high-torque.jelen.dk" />
            <textevent timeoffset="120" duration="35" message="Peter Schep (EF Pro Cycling) starts riders with '60 rpms at 80 to 85% of your FTP'. Four 4-min reps with rest as long as the rep are John Wakefield's. Putting them together is our choice." />
            <textevent timeoffset="240" duration="25" message="Same reps and power as weeks 1-2, lower cadence: 50-60 rpm instead of 60-65. More force on every pedal stroke." />
            <textevent timeoffset="420" duration="25" message="All intervals are SEATED. Standing lets you use body weight on the pedals, bypassing the forced muscular contraction that makes this training work." />
            <textevent timeoffset="600" duration="25" message="Target cadence: 50-60 rpm. In ERG mode, just drop to this cadence. The trainer raises the resistance to hold the power." />
            <textevent timeoffset="720" duration="20" message="If your knees ache at any point during an interval, stop the interval. Don't push through knee discomfort." />
            <textevent timeoffset="820" duration="20" message="Controlled: steady, below your threshold. Heavy legs are normal." />
        </Warmup>

        <SteadyState Duration="240" Power="0.80" Cadence="55">
            <textevent timeoffset="10" message="Interval 1. 80-85% FTP, 50-60 rpm. STAY SEATED." />
            <textevent timeoffset="120" message="Halfway. Steady, below threshold." />
            <textevent timeoffset="210" message="30 seconds left in this interval." />
        </SteadyState>

        <SteadyState Duration="240" Power="0.50">
            <textevent timeoffset="10" message="Recovery. Easy spin at whatever cadence feels natural." />
            <textevent timeoffset="60" duration="25" message="At the same power, 50-60 rpm means roughly 1.5 to 1.8 times the force on each pedal stroke of 90 rpm. That extra force per stroke is the point of torque work." />
            <textevent timeoffset="150" duration="30" message="Henderson: 'the first thing I would do is a little bit of tempo … three to five minute efforts that are more like 75 to 85% of threshold power at say 50 to 60 rpm.'" />
        </SteadyState>

        <SteadyState Duration="240" Power="0.80" Cadence="55">
            <textevent timeoffset="10" message="Interval 2. 80-85% FTP, 50-60 rpm. STAY SEATED." />
            <textevent timeoffset="120" message="Halfway. Smooth pedal strokes." />
            <textevent timeoffset="210" message="30 seconds left in this interval." />
        </SteadyState>

        <SteadyState Duration="240" Power="0.50">
            <textevent timeoffset="10" message="Recovery. Spin freely." />
            <textevent timeoffset="40" duration="30" message="If your cadence sags below target, lower the workout intensity (FTP bias) rather than grinding. These weeks are the low-stakes place to learn ERG at low cadence." />
            <textevent timeoffset="160" message="Two down, two to go. Knees still feeling fine?" />
        </SteadyState>

        <SteadyState Duration="240" Power="0.80" Cadence="55">
            <textevent timeoffset="10" message="Interval 3. 80-85% FTP, 50-60 rpm. STAY SEATED." />
            <textevent timeoffset="120" message="Halfway. Heavy legs are normal." />
            <textevent timeoffset="210" message="30 seconds left in this interval." />
        </SteadyState>

        <SteadyState Duration="240" Power="0.50">
            <textevent timeoffset="10" message="Recovery. Three down, one to go." />
            <textevent timeoffset="160" duration="20" message="If your knees are aching, skip the final interval. Protecting the joints is always the right call on torque work." />
        </SteadyState>

        <SteadyState Duration="240" Power="0.80" Cadence="55">
            <textevent timeoffset="10" message="Final interval. 80-85% FTP, 50-60 rpm. STAY SEATED." />
            <textevent timeoffset="120" duration="20" message="Halfway. Last effort. Keep the cadence low and the pedal stroke smooth." />
            <textevent timeoffset="210" message="30 seconds. Finish it." />
        </SteadyState>

        <Cooldown Duration="600" PowerLow="0.50" PowerHigh="0.40">
            <textevent timeoffset="30" message="Cool down. Spin easy at normal cadence." />
            <textevent timeoffset="120" duration="20" message="Note how your knees feel. Any ache or tightness? If yes, skip the next torque session and monitor." />
            <textevent timeoffset="270" duration="30" message="Our rule for moving on: after two of these sessions, your knees were fine, you held the cadence, and you weren't wrecked the next day. Otherwise repeat this one." />
            <textevent timeoffset="420" duration="25" message="Our rule: no torque sessions on back-to-back days. CTS coach Jim Rutberg says beginner and intermediate riders should separate them by at least a full day." />
        </Cooldown>
    </workout>
</workout_file>
